The code statements that lead to the bug are:
ipv6_del_addr(ifp);
net_info_ratelimited("%s: Failed to add prefix route for address %pI6c; dropping\n",
                     idev->dev->name, &ifp->addr);
Reason:
`ipv6_del_addr(ifp)` can free the `ifp` structure and its associated data (e.g., `ifp->addr`). The subsequent `net_info_ratelimited()` call then accesses `&ifp->addr`, which is a use-after-free (UaF). The warning is delivered too late, after the memory has been freed.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
net_info_ratelimited("%s: Failed to add prefix route for address %pI6c; dropping\n",
                     idev->dev->name, &ifp->addr);
in6_ifa_hold(ifp);
ipv6_del_addr(ifp);
Reason:
The warning is moved before `ipv6_del_addr()`, ensuring that `ifp` and its fields (like `ifp->addr`) are still valid when accessed. `in6_ifa_hold()` is still called to take an extra reference before the deletion, but the critical change is that the warning no longer follows the freeing call. This eliminates the use-after-freed memory access.

